%I A090518 #12 Oct 06 2026 17:31:54
%S A090518 5,11,83,769,3571,52631,1428571,3703703,83333333,769230769,5882352941,
%T A090518 13513513513,588235294117,7142857142857,12195121951219,
%U A090518 151515151515151,2777777777777777,22727272727272727,1111111111111111111
%N A090518 Primes arising in A090517, or 0 if A090517(n) = 0.
%C A090518 Conjecture: No term is zero.
%C A090518 The conjecture is true. For every n >= 1, k = 5*10^(n-1) gives floor(10^n/k) = 2, which is prime. Hence the set of admissible k is nonempty, so A090517(n) is well-defined. The corresponding quotient A090518(n) is prime by definition and therefore nonzero. - _Lien-Hung Su_, Oct 01 2026
%H A090518 Lien-Hung Su, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23082340">Nonvanishing of Least-Witness Prime Floor Quotients for Decimal Powers</a>, 2026.
%o A090518 (PARI) A090518(n)={ local(k,tenn) ; k=1 ; tenn=10^n ; while(1, if( isprime(floor(tenn/k)), return(floor(tenn/k)) ) ; k++ ; ) ; }
%o A090518 { for(n=1,40, print1(A090518(n),",") ; ) } \\ _R. J. Mathar_, Nov 19 2006
%Y A090518 Cf. A090517, A090519, A090520.
